---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Efficient AI & Inverse Problems - François Pitié" currentSection="research">
  <div class="post">
    <header class="post-header mb-8">
      <div class="mb-2">
        <a href="/research" class="text-xs font-mono text-[var(--theme-link)] hover:text-[var(--theme-accent)]">&larr; back to research</a>
      </div>
      <h1 class="post-title">Efficient AI &amp; Inverse Problems</h1>
      <p class="desc text-xs sm:text-sm text-[var(--theme-muted)] mt-1.5 pb-3 border-b border-[var(--theme-border)]">
        Reusable lightweight denoisers, linear inverse problems, and classical signal processing backbones.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="space-y-10">
      <!-- Overview -->
      <section class="space-y-3 text-sm sm:text-base text-[var(--theme-fg)] leading-relaxed">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-light tracking-tight text-[var(--theme-heading)] pb-1.5 border-b border-[var(--theme-border)]">
          Overview
        </h2>
        <p>
          State-of-the-art vision models often rely on massive neural networks that require heavy GPU infrastructure and are slow to run. For camera pipelines and practical creative tools, smaller models that run fast are much more practical.
        </p>
        <p>
          My work in this area focuses on combining classical signal processing theory with compact learned models. Instead of training monolithic models from scratch, we build reusable, lightweight components that can handle multiple imaging tasks efficiently.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Multi-Task Denoisers for Linear Inverse Problems -->
      <section class="space-y-3 text-sm sm:text-base text-[var(--theme-fg)] leading-relaxed">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-light tracking-tight text-[var(--theme-heading)] pb-1.5 border-b border-[var(--theme-border)]">
          Multi-Task Denoisers for Inverse Problems
        </h2>
        <p>
          Many imaging tasks involve recovering an image from incomplete or degraded measurements—such as super-resolution, demosaicing, and inpainting. These are classic <em>inverse problems</em>. A common approach is to use a learned image denoiser as a prior inside an iterative reconstruction loop. However, standard methods typically train a separate denoiser for each task.
        </p>
        <p>
          In work presented at <strong>CVMP 2025</strong> with Clément Bled, we showed that a single lightweight denoiser can serve as a shared prior across multiple inverse problems. By fine-tuning the denoiser within the iterative reconstruction process across several tasks simultaneously, the model learns a general image prior rather than overfitting to one type of degradation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Evaluated across six diverse inverse problems, this unified denoiser achieved an average <strong>+1.34 dB PSNR improvement</strong> over single-task baselines while also converging in fewer iterations.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Classical Backbones & Wiener Filtering -->
      <section class="space-y-3 text-sm sm:text-base text-[var(--theme-fg)] leading-relaxed">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-light tracking-tight text-[var(--theme-heading)] pb-1.5 border-b border-[var(--theme-border)]">
          Lightweight Denoising with Classical Backbones
        </h2>
        <p>
          For video and image denoising, large neural networks often have too much latency for live or high-throughput workflows.
        </p>
        <p>
          We investigated how classical Wiener filtering can be paired with small neural networks to estimate filter parameters. Presented at <strong>IEEE ICIP 2023</strong> and <strong>IEEE ICME 2024</strong> (and published in <em>Frontiers in Signal Processing</em>), this hybrid approach matches the denoising quality of large convolutional models while running with a tiny fraction of the parameters and compute.
        </p>
      </section>

